  "body": "What happens: the maintainer starts the simulation and gets this status line (2026-11-16, several times):\n\n```\n! generated 2D Adaptive1: 4269 moves (0 plunge(s), 3 level(s), 1617 arc(s)), 0 warning(s) - arc output: of 789 fitted arcs, 169 could not be proved inside the cutter-centre area, 85 would add a kink and 2 would cross another feed - those stay chords - Carried stock: the operation's top Z20.000 is below the material over its region (Z22.000) - the first descent starts over that material and feeds down; the first level engages 2.000 mm | traced playback refused: 2D Contour1: no toolpath yet - generate it first - the sheet stands\n```\n\n2D Adaptive1 has a toolpath. 2D Contour1 has none. The traced playback of the stock is refused for the whole setup, and the simulation shows no stock removal.\n\nThree faults:\n\n1. One operation without a toolpath stops the stock playback of each operation before it.\n2. The refusal is at the end of a long line, after the notes of a different operation. The line starts with `!`, thus the notes of 2D Adaptive1 look like an error. They are notes.\n3. The words \"traced playback\" and \"the sheet stands\" are not plain English for a user.\n\nExpected:\n\n- The stock playback runs for each operation in program order up to the first operation that has no toolpath. It does not skip an operation and continue: the stock after a skipped operation is wrong.\n- A separate, short message names the operation where the playback stops and what to do: \"Stock simulation stops before 2D Contour1: it has no toolpath. Generate it to see the rest.\"\n- The generation notes of an operation do not show as an error when the operation has 0 warnings.\n\nSafety: the stock judgment that can block the post does not change. This issue changes only what the playback shows and the message text. An operation without a toolpath does not post, as today.\n\nDone means: a setup with a generated operation followed by an operation without a toolpath plays the stock removal of the first one, and a test pins that. A test pins the new message text. A test pins that the notes of a clean operation do not carry the error mark.\n",
